From the near side, Earth hangs in nearly the same place all month. It looks about four times wider than the full Moon does from Earth. You would see it go through phases: full Earth, half Earth, thin crescent Earth.

Sometimes Earth passes in front of the Sun. Then the whole Earth glows with a thin red ring around its edge. That ring is every sunrise and sunset on Earth happening at once.

{{story:moon-phases}}

Jump on a concrete floor and you stop in a few hundredths of a second. That sudden stop jolts your bones and wastes energy. On a trampoline, you slow down over a much longer time, perhaps a fifth of a second, as the mat sinks.

That longer, softer stop means your legs have more time to push. It's the same reason a high jumper lands on a thick mat and a cricketer pulls the hands back when catching a fast ball. Spreading out a stop makes it gentler.

{{story:wild-kangaroo}}

Sailors and fishermen had another trick. They rubbed linseed oil or wax into heavy cotton or linen. The oil filled the gaps between the threads and made water bead up and roll off. Their coats, called oilskins, were often yellow so that a sailor who fell overboard was easier to spot.

Waxed cotton jackets are still made today. They can be re-waxed when they start to let water in.

{{story:lotus}}

Empathy-like behaviour shows up in other animals. In a 2011 experiment in the USA, rats learned to open a little door to free a trapped cage-mate. Many did this before eating chocolate that was also on offer, and some saved chocolate for the rat they had freed.

{{story:elephants-talk}}

Asian [[@elephants-talk|elephants]] in Thailand have been seen comforting upset companions, touching them with their trunks and making soft chirping sounds. Chimpanzees hug and groom friends who have lost a fight. Dogs often come close when their owners cry.

Chinese mathematicians were using decimal fractions over 1,500 years ago, and Arab mathematicians such as al-Kashi in Samarkand used them with great skill in the 1400s. The idea took longer to reach ordinary people.

In 1585, a Flemish engineer named Simon Stevin published a short booklet called The Tenth. He argued that everyone, from merchants to soldiers, should use decimal fractions because they were so much easier. He even suggested decimal money and measures. His way of writing decimals was awkward, with little circled numbers, and the dot came into use later. Different countries chose differently. India, the USA and Britain use a dot, while much of Europe and South America uses a comma.

{{story:olympics}}

The most famous cube net looks like a cross: four squares in a column, with one square on each side of the second square. Fold the side squares up and the top square over, and you have a cube.

There are 10 others. Some look like a staircase. Some have three squares in a row with the rest hanging off at odd places. Mathematicians call any shape made of six joined squares a hexomino. There are 35 hexominoes in all, but only 11 are cube nets. A good test is to picture folding one face at a time and check that no two faces end up in the same place.

{{story:paper-made}}

In the 1840s, the English mathematician Ada Lovelace studied a machine designed by her friend [[@charles-babbage|Charles Babbage]], called the Analytical Engine. It was never fully built. In 1843 she published long notes on it, including a step-by-step method for the machine to calculate a series of special numbers. Many people call this the first published computer program. She also guessed that such machines might one day handle music and pictures as well as numbers.

In France, Sophie Germain wasn't allowed to study at university. She got lecture notes from friends and sent her work to the great mathematician Carl Friedrich Gauss under the name "Monsieur LeBlanc". When Gauss found out, he wrote to praise her. Her work on how surfaces vibrate helped engineers understand how structures bend.

{{story:apollo-moon}}

लड़कों में जैसे-जैसे स्वर-यंत्र बढ़ता है, उसका अगला हिस्सा ज़्यादा बाहर निकल आता है। यह अक्सर गले में एक उभार की तरह दिखता है, जिसे कंठमणि या ‘ऐडम्स ऐपल’ कहते हैं। यह हर किसी के होता है। लड़कियों और छोटे बच्चों में स्वर-यंत्र छोटा होता है और सामने का कोण चौड़ा, इसलिए यह मुश्किल से दिखता है।

बच्चे के स्वर-रज्जु बहुत छोटे होते हैं, अक्सर एक सेंटीमीटर से भी कम। बड़े पुरुषों में ये लगभग 1.5 से 2.5 सेमी के होते हैं, यानी मोटे तौर पर दस रुपये के सिक्के की चौड़ाई जितने। बड़ी महिलाओं में थोड़े छोटे।

{{story:sound-travel}}

ऊब का [[@focus|ध्यान]] से गहरा रिश्ता है। जब कोई काम बहुत आसान हो, तो ध्यान उससे फिसल जाता है। जब काम बहुत कठिन हो, या समझ में न आए, तो ध्यान हार मान लेता है। दोनों ही उबाऊ लग सकते हैं। शोधकर्ताओं ने यह भी पाया है कि जब लोगों को लगता है कि उनके पास कोई चारा नहीं, तब वे बोर होते हैं, जैसे लंबी कतार में इंतज़ार करते हुए, या कोई ऐसा भाषण सुनते हुए जिसे उन्होंने चुना नहीं।

ऊब समय को भी मोड़ देती है। बोर होते समय हर मिनट लंबा लगता है, क्योंकि तुम्हारा ध्यान बार-बार घड़ी पर जाता है। फिर भी जब तुम किसी उबाऊ हफ़्ते को पलटकर देखते हो, तो वह जल्दी बीता लगता है, क्योंकि उसमें ऐसा कुछ हुआ ही नहीं जिसे याददाश्त पकड़कर रख सके।

{{story:charles-dickens}}

1990 के दशक में अमेरिका के MIT में वैज्ञानिक ऐन ग्रेबील और उनकी टीम ने चूहों को चॉकलेट के लिए एक आसान भूलभुलैया में दौड़ना सिखाया। शुरू में चूहों के दिमाग का एक गहरा हिस्सा, जिसे बेसल गैंग्लिया कहते हैं, पूरी भूलभुलैया में व्यस्त रहता था। जैसे-जैसे चूहे सीखते गए, [[neuron|तंत्रिका कोशिकाओं]] की वह हलचल शुरुआत और आखिर में सिमट गई। बीच में दिमाग आराम कर सकता था।

आदतों का यही फ़ायदा है। ये मेहनत बचाती हैं। जब शरीर ऑटोपायलट पर [[@teeth|दाँत]] ब्रश कर रहा होता है, तब सोचने वाला दिमाग दिन की योजना बनाने के लिए आज़ाद रहता है। मनोवैज्ञानिक वेंडी वुड के एक अध्ययन में पाया गया कि लोग रोज़ जो करते हैं, उसका लगभग 40 [[percentage|प्रतिशत]] आदत से होता है, आम तौर पर किसी और चीज़ के बारे में सोचते हुए।

{{story:toothbrush}}

In 1997, IBM's Deep Blue computer beat world [[@chess-india|chess]] champion Garry Kasparov in a match. It worked mostly by checking enormous numbers of possible moves very fast.

{{story:chess-champions}}

The ancient board game Go has far more possible positions than chess, and was thought too hard for computers. In 2016, a program called AlphaGo, which learned partly by playing millions of games against itself, beat Lee Sedol, one of the world's best players.

On a hot day in Delhi or Phoenix, cotton traps a lot of body heat. Your body releases heat partly as [[infrared|infrared]] light. Researchers at Stanford University and other labs have made fabrics that let infrared light pass through but still look like normal cloth.

Other fabrics reflect sunlight. Some are inspired by the shiny hairs of the Saharan silver ant, which reflect sunlight and help it survive scorching sand. Engineers think such clothes could help outdoor workers in India's heatwaves, where cooling can protect health.

{{story:spacesuits}}

The Ganga and Brahmaputra carry silt down from the Himalayas, more than a billion tonnes of it each year. Where they slow down near the sea, the mud settles and builds land. Over thousands of years, this has made the largest delta in the world.

Bangladesh has hundreds of rivers, and boats are a normal way to travel. The soil is so rich that farmers can grow two or three rice crops a year. In the south lies part of the Sundarbans, the largest mangrove forest on Earth, shared with India and home to the Bengal tiger. Some families keep homes and schools on boats in the flood season, and charities have run floating schools that sail from village to village to pick up children.

{{story:deltas}}

The south tower stands in open water. Divers had to work in the strong current to help build a huge concrete wall around the spot, a bit like a giant bucket. Water was pumped out so crews could pour the foundation on solid rock.

Then the steel towers rose, piece by piece, until they reached 227 metres above the water. That is roughly as tall as a 65-storey building. The [[@steel|steel]] came by train from Pennsylvania, on the other side of the country.

{{story:bridges}}

The main shrine faces east, toward the sea, so the first light of morning falls on it. A smaller Shiva shrine faces west, toward the land. Between them lies a shrine to Vishnu, shown lying down in deep rest. This Vishnu is carved from the bedrock itself, and the temple was built around him.

Rows of stone bulls, Nandi, line the walls of the courtyard. Many have been worn almost smooth by centuries of salty wind.

{{story:tsunami}}

About 4,500 years ago, one of the world's first great [[civilisation|civilisations]] grew along the Indus River and its neighbours. Its people built cities with straight streets, brick houses, [[@oddity-ancient-toilets|covered drains]] and huge granaries. You can explore them in [[@indus-cities|Indus cities]].

Harappa and Mohenjo-daro are in Pakistan. Dholavira, Lothal and Rakhigarhi are in India. [[archaeologist|Archaeologists]] from both countries study the same civilisation, sometimes reading the same mysterious, still undeciphered script on the same kind of small stone seals.

{{story:indus-cities}}

Around 570 CE, a Persian physician is said to have brought the Panchatantra from India to the court of the Persian king Khusrau. It was translated into the language of [[@ancient-persia|Persia]], then into Arabic, where it became *Kalila wa Dimna*, named after two jackals. From there it spread into Hebrew, Latin, Spanish and many more languages.

When the French poet Jean de La Fontaine published more fables in 1678, he mentioned an Indian sage, "Pilpay", as a source. Some of the fables we think of as Western, alongside those of [[@aesop|Aesop]], have Indian cousins.

{{story:puppetry}}

In one well-known tale, the Bodhisatta is the leader of 80,000 monkeys living in a [[@mango-tree|mango]] tree beside the Ganga. A king's archers surround the tree at night. The monkey king leaps across the river, ties a long vine to a tree on the far bank and to his own body, and stretches himself into a living bridge. His monkeys cross over his back to safety.

He is badly hurt, and the king, watching, is deeply moved. He learns what a real leader does for his people. This story, the Mahakapi Jataka, is carved on a stone medallion from Bharhut, now in the Indian Museum in Kolkata, and also appears in the paintings at [[@ajanta-colours|Ajanta]].

{{story:sri-lanka}}

लोग हज़ारों सालों से चिपचिपी चीज़ें चबाते आ रहे हैं। स्कैंडिनेविया में [[archaeologist|पुरातत्वविदों]] को भोज-पत्र के पेड़ की छाल के तारकोल के लौंदे मिले हैं, जिन पर इंसानी दाँतों के निशान हैं। यह काला, लिसलिसा तारकोल छाल को गर्म करके बनता था। कुछ लौंदे लगभग 10,000 साल पुराने हैं। लोग शायद इसे नरम करने के लिए चबाते थे, ताकि [[@stone-age|पाषाण युग]] के औज़ार जोड़ने में गोंद की तरह इस्तेमाल कर सकें।

2019 में डेनमार्क के वैज्ञानिकों ने सिल्थोल्म नाम की जगह पर मिले चबाए हुए तारकोल के एक लौंदे की जाँच की, जो 5,700 साल पुराना था। उसमें अब तक फँसी लार से उन्होंने उस लड़की का पूरा [[@dna|डीएनए]] पढ़ लिया। उन्होंने अंदाज़ा लगाया कि उसकी त्वचा और बाल शायद गहरे रंग के थे और आँखें नीली। यहाँ तक कि उसमें बत्तख़ और हेज़लनट के खाने के निशान भी मिले।

{{story:dna}}

दालों का एक छिपा हुआ साथी है। उनकी जड़ों पर छोटी, गोल गाँठें उगती हैं, जिन्हें नॉड्यूल कहते हैं, और उनके अंदर राइज़ोबिया नाम के [[bacteria|बैक्टीरिया]] रहते हैं। ये बैक्टीरिया हवा से नाइट्रोजन गैस लेते हैं, जिसे पौधे सीधे इस्तेमाल नहीं कर सकते, और उसे ऐसे रूप में बदल देते हैं जो पौधों के काम आए।

{{story:soil-made}}

नाइट्रोजन उन मुख्य चीज़ों में से है जो पौधों को चाहिए, और कई [[fertiliser|खादों]] का मुख्य हिस्सा भी यही है। दाल की फ़सल के बाद उस नाइट्रोजन का कुछ हिस्सा अगली फ़सल के लिए [[@soil-made|मिट्टी]] में बचा रहता है। भारत के किसान सदियों से अनाज के साथ बारी-बारी से दालें बोते आए हैं।
